# `s += pair[0] + pair[1]`: that both are numbers is a guess, and its stretch ends behind the inner sum: the arms meet in front of the outer one, and the closed arm brings a box. So the sum stays boxed: int32s are added as int32s.
# built on doubles, both numbers were unboxed, added, and the sum asked whether it is an int32 to be boxed for the meeting: eleven instructions more
# The list leaves the program, so that Link has seen nothing of what comes out of it (LinkTables::resultsOfReadsByKey).
# Where the loop is built twice each pair is asked in the turn for its storage and the typed copy adds raw numbers; the other copy keeps this form: NEVER is asked of the loop built once.
# f1 = both. Control: f2 = halved: `(pair[0] + pair[1]) * 0.5`: the product stands in the stretch and takes the sum raw, as ever
